  "strategy": "I'm going to implement a multi-modal optimization strategy that combines elements of simulated annealing in frequency domain and magnitude minimization in time domain. This will allow us to systematically explore different sequence topologies and potentially find sequences that achieve lower objective values than the parent solution.\n\nKey design components:\n1. Frequency domain optimization using Fourier transforms to control the autocorrelation peak\n2. Time domain optimization to enforce gradient-based improvements\n3. Random walk mode for escaping local optima\n4. Multi-length exploration to prevent being trapped by initial sequence length\n5. Aggressive spectral control (tilting, clipping) to start from strong bases\n\nHere's the step-by-step plan:\n\n1. Initial Setup and Security Checks:\n   - Start by loading the parent sequence if available\n   - Perform initial validation: check if sequence is non-empty and composed of finite floating point numbers\n\n2. Sequence Representation:\n   - Represent sequences as NumPy arrays with exact length\n   - Ensure all values are non-negative and clipped to [0, 1000]\n\n3. Objective Function Implementation:\n   - We'll implement the same objective function used by the evaluator\n   - This involves computing the full linear convolution and minimizing the ratio\n\n4. Fourier-Magnitude Optimization:\n   - Compute Fourier transform of initial sequence\n   - Apply target magnitude shaping (spectral tilting, soft clipping)\n   - Transform back to time domain and adjust for non-negativity\n\n5. Time-Domain Optimization:\n   - Use a gradient-based approach focusing on autocorrelation peaks\n   - Iteratively adjust coefficients to flatten the autocorrelation envelope\n\n6. Random Walk Mode:\n   - Occasionally inject random fluctuations to prevent convergence to suboptimal solutions\n   - After each fluctuation, normalize the sequence and re-optimze\n\n7. Size Management:\n   - Start from parent length if available, otherwise fixed length\n   - Expand to multiples of base length (N=400,800,1100...) until the time budget is exhausted\n\n8. Final Validation:\n   - Always include the original best candidate in case of issues\n   - Format return value as a list of floats\n\nThe approach will dynamically select between Fourier shaping and time-domain optimization based on the current solution's characteristics and age. This hybrid strategy aims to find structure-optimal solutions by leveraging both spectral and temporal properties.\n\nLet me implement this with the aggressive hyperparameter sweeps from the original:",
